// How Sliding Window works
A window is a range within an array or string (continuous segment).
Example window on array: A[l...r]
Example window on string: s[l...r]

The window slides by moving the left or right pointers.
⭐ Sliding Window Categories

There are two main types:

A. Fixed-size Sliding Window

Used when the problem tells you window size k.

👉 Common problems:

Max sum of subarray of size k

First negative number in window size k

Count occurrences of anagrams (size = pattern length)

How it works

Expand window until size == k

Record answer

Slide by:

Remove left element

Add new right element

Example (Array):

Max sum of subarray of size k = 3

arr = [2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2]
k = 3


Window movements:

[2,1,5] → sum = 8
   [1,5,1] → sum = 7
      [5,1,3] → sum = 9  <-- max
         [1,3,2] → sum = 6


Time = O(n)

B. Variable-size Sliding Window

Used when:

We don’t know window size

Problem gives a condition, and window grows/shrinks accordingly

👉 Common problems:

Longest substring without repeating characters

Longest substring with at most K distinct chars

Smallest subarray with sum ≥ target

Minimum window substring

How it works

This is where SDET interviewers test your logic.

Rules:

Expand the right pointer to include characters/elements

If condition breaks, shrink from left

Track longest or shortest window depending on problem

Example (String):

Longest substring without repeating characters

s = "abcabcbb"


Use HashSet or Map.

Window growth:

a → ok
ab → ok
abc → ok
abca → 'a' repeats → shrink left until valid


Final answer = "abc" → length 3

🎯 How Sliding Window Differs for Arrays vs Strings
Concept	Arrays	Strings
Storage	Use running sum or counters	Use HashMap/Array to track characters
Condition checks	numeric comparisons	duplicate chars, distinct chars
Complexity	O(n)	O(n)

Logic is the same—only tracking method changes.

💡 FULL TEMPLATE FOR SLIDING WINDOW (Variable Size)
Java Template
int left = 0;
for (int right = 0; right < s.length(); right++) {

    // expand window by including s.charAt(right)

    while (/* condition violated */) {
        // shrink window
        left++;
    }

    // update answer
}

📌 Let’s Understand Through 2 Classic Problems
Problem 1: Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters
Logic

Expand right pointer

Keep chars in map

If repeat → shrink from left